ential Access"],"behavior":"GruntHTTP.exe (Covenant C2 agent) executed from pgustavo's Desktop on WORKSTATION5, created temporary PowerShell files, and connected to lsass and wkssvc named pipes to extract credentials.","slot":0,"reasoning":"The operator used GruntHTTP.exe to connect to the lsass named pipe, which is the primary mechanism for accessing credential material stored in LSASS process memory. While the process also created temporary PowerShell files and connected to wkssvc, the objective was credential extraction from LSASS, not general IPC abuse or process injection.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E018","field":"PipeName","quote":"\\lsass","ok":true},{"event_id":"E018","field":"Image","quote":"C:\\Users\\pgustavo\\Desktop\\GruntHTTP.exe","ok":true},{"event_id":"E019","field":"PipeName","quote":"\\wkssvc","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1134","name":"Access Token Manipulation","parent":null,"tactics":["Stealth","Privilege Escalation"],"behavior":"Security token permissions were modified for pgustavo via wsmprovhost.exe process on WORKSTATION6, enabling privilege escalation or credential manipulation.","slot":2,"reasoning":"The event shows direct modification of security token permissions by pgustavo through wsmprovhost.exe, which is characteristic of access token manipulation for privilege escalation. While the specific sub-technique cannot be determined from the single audit event (which lacks API-level detail about duplication, impersonation, or parent PID spoofing), the core behavior\u2014token permission changes enabling privilege escalation\u2014maps to the parent technique T1134.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E046","field":"ObjectType","quote":"Token","ok":true},{"event_id":"E046","field":"ProcessName","quote":"C:\\Windows\\System32\\wsmprovhost.exe","ok":true},{"event_id":"E046","field":"SubjectUserName","quote":"pgustavo","ok":true}],"attempts":1}],"unverified":[],"declined":[],"hypotheses":[{"behavior":"GruntHTTP.exe (Covenant C2 agent) execute